<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <application
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/AppTheme"

        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"

        android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config"
        android:usesCleartextTraffic="false">

        <activity
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|locale|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|uiMode|navigation"
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:label="@string/title_activity_main"
            android:theme="@style/AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:exported="true">

            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!--
                OAuth return path. Supabase redirects the system browser to
                exactly `gomsinlog://auth/callback` (see authRedirectUrl() in
                src/lib/platform.ts), and @capacitor/app hands that URL to
                src/lib/deepLinks.ts.

                scheme + host + path are all pinned. A filter with only
                android:scheme would claim EVERY `gomsinlog://...` URL, so any
                other app (or a web page) could hand this activity an
                arbitrary path; narrowing it to the single route we actually
                serve removes that surface. `android:path` is an exact match,
                not a prefix.
            -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                <data
                    android:scheme="gomsinlog"
                    android:host="auth"
                    android:path="/callback" />
            </intent-filter>

        </activity>

        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/file_paths"></meta-data>
        </provider>
    </application>

    <!--
        Permissions.

        Every entry below is justified by code in this repository. Nothing is
        declared "just in case": an unused permission is both a Play review
        finding and a real widening of what a compromised WebView could reach.

        INTERNET
            Supabase REST/Realtime/Storage. Required by the bridge itself.

        ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE
            Required for `navigator.onLine` to mean anything in the WebView.
            Chromium's network-change notifier can only read connectivity through
            ConnectivityManager, which is guarded by this permission; undeclared,
            the query is refused and Chromium reports CONNECTION_NONE, so
            `navigator.onLine` is permanently FALSE on a perfectly connected
            device.

            That is not cosmetic in this app. src/lib/useOnlineStatus.ts TRUSTS a
            `false` and offline is a PRE-EMPTIVE read-only mode, so every write
            control disables itself and TodayLogWidget.handlePost refuses to save
            with OFFLINE_READONLY_MESSAGE while OfflineBanner says the connection
            is gone. Writing a diary entry therefore reported "no internet" on a
            device that was online, which is the symptom the product owner
            reported.

            Normal (install-time) permission: no runtime prompt, no user-visible
            dialog, no data leaves the device because of it.

        NOT declared since 2026-08-21: RECORD_AUDIO + MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS
            The voice recorder left the composer with the PRODUCT_V3 §12.4
            upload gate (photo-only until the P6 encrypted media foundation), so
            no code calls getUserMedia({ audio }) any more. A declared
            microphone permission with no feature behind it is exactly what a
            store reviewer and a privacy audit flag; both return together when
            P6 re-admits voice, alongside src/lib/nativePermissions.ts, which
            keeps the rationale copy parked for that day.

        NOT declared: android.permission.CAMERA
            TodayLogWidget's "지금찍기" button sets capture="environment" on the
            file input. BridgeWebChromeClient.onShowFileChooser handles that by
            launching MediaStore.ACTION_IMAGE_CAPTURE, so the *system camera app*
            takes the photo and returns a content:// URI; this process never
            opens the camera device. And isMediaCaptureSupported()
            (BridgeWebChromeClient.java:307-313) treats "CAMERA is not declared
            at all" as permitted, so declaring CAMERA would ADD a runtime
            prompt while removing nothing. Not declared, deliberately.

        NOT declared: contacts, location, tracking / advertising id,
        READ_MEDIA_IMAGES / READ_MEDIA_VIDEO / READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE
            Attachment picking goes through the system document picker, which
            returns a URI the app is already granted. No code reads the media
            store, and no code reads contacts, location or an advertising id.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />
</manifest>
